\begin{proof}
Let $X$ be a linearly ordered set with its order topology. We use the following elementary fact: if $x\in B$, where $B$ is a basic order-topology neighborhood of $x$, then there is a basic neighborhood $V$ of $x$ such that $\overline V\subseteq B$. Indeed, for $B=(a,b)$ choose points strictly between $a,x$ and $x,b$ whenever they exist; if one side has no such point, use the corresponding half-open basic interval, and if neither side has such a point then $\{x\}$ is open. The same argument applies to the basic rays $[\min X,b)$ and $(a,\max X]$.

Now let $U$ be open and let $x\in U$. By the definition of the order topology, there is a basic neighborhood $B$ with $x\in B\subseteq U$. By the fact above, choose an open neighborhood $V$ of $x$ with $\overline V\subseteq B\subseteq U$. Thus every point and every open neighborhood of that point admit an open neighborhood whose closure lies in the original neighborhood. Hence $X$ is regular.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}
Let $X$ be a linearly ordered set equipped with the order topology. First, $X$ is $T_1$: for each $x\in X$, the complement of $\{x\}$ is a union of open rays and intervals, hence is open.

We prove regularity. Let $x\in U$, with $U$ open. Choose a basic order-topology neighborhood $B$ such that $x\in B\subseteq U$. Basic neighborhoods are open intervals and, when an endpoint exists, the corresponding half-open rays.

We claim that $B$ contains an open neighborhood $V$ of $x$ whose closure is contained in $B$. If $B=(a,b)$ and there are $c,d$ with $a<c<x<d<b$, take $V=(c,d)$; then $\overline V\subseteq[c,d]\subseteq(a,b)$. If only the left (respectively right) interior point exists, use $(c,x]$ (respectively $[x,d)$), which is a basic open neighborhood in the relevant order-topology situation, and its closure is contained in $[c,x]\subseteq(a,b)$ (respectively $[x,d]\subseteq(a,b)$). If neither exists, then $(a,b)=\{x\}$, so $V=\{x\}$ works. For a basic ray, the same construction is made only on its finite-endpoint side; if no interior point exists, the ray itself is a singleton.

Therefore there is an open $V$ with $x\in V$ and $\overline V\subseteq B\subseteq U$. Hence $X$ is regular.
\end{proof}
